# {{name}}

Built with [TheoKit](https://theokit.dev) — **Build the app your agent lives in.**

This is the default template: a **working agent chat**, end to end. The thread
streams real replies, the tools really run, and the approval prompt really gates
the side-effecting one. Run it and you are talking to your agent.

## Getting Started

```bash
# 1. Install dependencies
pnpm install

# 2. Point the agent at a provider (OpenRouter, Anthropic, or OpenAI)
cp .env.example .env && $EDITOR .env

# 3. Start the dev server (HMR)
pnpm dev
```

Open the app and chat. Edit `src/server/agents/chat.ts` to change the model, add tools, or
swap the persona — `@theokit/sdk` is the runtime and resolves the provider from
your environment.

## Project Structure

```
{{name}}/
├── src/
│   ├── app/                # Frontend — the only half that ships to a browser
│   │   ├── page.tsx        #   the `/` route — composition root
│   │   ├── layout.tsx      #   root layout
│   │   ├── components/     #   presentational UI
│   │   ├── hooks/          #   custom hooks — where state lives
│   │   └── lib/            #   app modules / config
│   ├── server/             # Backend — everything that never leaves the machine
│   │   ├── agents/         #   the agent, and what composes it
│   │   │   ├── chat.ts     #     the agent → POST /api/agents/chat
│   │   │   ├── prompts/    #     system prompts / personas
│   │   │   ├── tools/      #     tools the agent can call (weather, current-time, …)
│   │   │   ├── skills/     #     procedures the model loads on demand
│   │   │   └── hooks/      #     lifecycle interception (audit every tool call)
│   │   └── routes/health.ts  # GET /api/health
│   └── shared/agent.ts     # Branding (name, model, greeting) — one source of truth
├── .theokit/               # Agent context — DATA, edited without a rebuild
│   ├── THEO.md             #   facts true on every turn
│   ├── rules/              #   instructions scoped to file globs
│   └── personalities/      #   swappable system prompts (usePersonality)
├── docs/                   # ARCHITECTURE · CUSTOMIZATION · ENVIRONMENT
├── theo.config.ts          # TheoKit configuration — declares the three paths above
└── .env.example            # Environment variables
```

**Code and data are the two halves.** `src/server/agents/` is what you compile — agents, tools,
skills, hooks. `.theokit/` is what the agent reads at runtime — context, rules, personalities. You
change the second without a rebuild, and that is the point of the split: tone and domain facts move
at a different speed than code, and shipping a deploy to reword a system prompt is a bad trade.

| You want to change                  | Edit                       | Rebuild? |
| ----------------------------------- | -------------------------- | -------- |
| what the agent can DO               | `src/server/agents/tools/` | yes      |
| what it always knows                | `.theokit/THEO.md`         | no       |
| how it behaves near certain files   | `.theokit/rules/`          | no       |
| how it talks, switchable at runtime | `.theokit/personalities/`  | no       |

**Why agents live under `server/`.** An agent reads your API keys, calls tools that touch the
network, and runs where the server runs. Every rule that applies to `server/` applies to it, so it
is a part of the backend rather than a third thing beside it — and `src/app/` stays exactly what it
claims to be: the only code that reaches a browser.

The three paths are declared in `theo.config.ts`, not assumed by the framework. Move them wherever
your project wants and update that file; nothing else needs to know.

## Key Concepts

- **An agent is a file.** `src/server/agents/chat.ts` is served at `POST /api/agents/chat` —
  nothing to register. Add a second agent as another `src/server/agents/<name>.ts`.
- **`AgentBuilder`** is the authoring surface. The chain accumulates type-state, so
  `.build()` without `.model()` is a compile error, not a runtime surprise:

  ```ts
  export default AgentBuilder.create()
    .input(z.object({ message: z.string() }))
    .model('openrouter/openai/gpt-4o-mini')
    .system(BASE_INSTRUCTIONS)
    .tool(weatherTool)
    .approval('send_notification', { question: 'Send this notification?' })
    .skills([dailyBriefingSkill])
    .build()
  ```

- **A tool is a file too** — `tool('name').describe(…).input(z.object({…})).execute(…).build()`.
  Pure metadata plus a handler; it never calls an LLM.
- **The sub-folders under `src/server/agents/` are semantic, not routes.** `prompts/`, `tools/`,
  `skills/` and friends are skipped by the scanner, so `src/server/agents/tools/weather.ts`
  never becomes a phantom `/api/agents/tools/weather` endpoint.
- **`useAgent`** binds the client to the agent and hands you the transcript:
  `const { thread, send, status, reset, error } = useAgent('/api/agents/chat')`.
- **Human-in-the-loop.** `.approval(tool, …)` pauses the run before that tool and
  asks. Ask the agent to "notify me that …" to see it.
- **Styling is wired for you.** Tailwind v4 and `@theokit/ui` are detected and
  configured by the framework — there is no `tailwind.config.ts` to maintain.

Deeper detail lives in [`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`](./docs/ARCHITECTURE.md);
customization and environment variables have their own pages beside it.

## What is already installed

Two component libraries come with this template, and the split is by **what a component is for**,
not by how generic it looks:

| You need                                                | Import from       | Examples                                                                                                               |
| ------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Agent surfaces — chat, streaming, tool calls, approvals | **`@theokit/ui`** | `ChatThread`, `ChatMessage`, `ChatComposer`, `ToolCallCard`, `AgentStream`                                             |
| App chrome — shell, navigation, tables, forms, feedback | **`@usetheo/ui`** | `Sidebar`, `PageShell`, `DataTable`, `Select`, `FormField`, `Badge`, `EmptyState`, `Alert`, `Dialog`, `CommandPalette` |

This template uses a handful of them. The rest are installed and waiting — **check before you write
markup by hand.** A real app built on this scaffold hand-wrote around 600 lines of navigation, rows,
filters, badges and empty states that already existed in these two packages
(usetheokit/theokit#471).

The catalogue is `node_modules/@theokit/ui/llms.txt` — written to be read by an agent, and it
carries the routing table above in full.

## Adding a screen

Routing is file-based: a screen is a folder under `src/app/` with a `page.tsx`.

| You want             | Create                              | Serves              |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------------- |
| a `/settings` screen | `src/app/settings/page.tsx`         | `/settings`         |
| a nested screen      | `src/app/settings/billing/page.tsx` | `/settings/billing` |
| a dynamic screen     | `src/app/users/[id]/page.tsx`       | `/users/:id`        |

Or run `theokit generate page settings`, then link it from `src/app/components/Nav.tsx`.

## Other scaffolds

The same agent runs behind a terminal or desktop frontend — only the transport
changes. Scaffold them with `--surface=tui` (Ink) or `--surface=desktop` (Tauri).

Want the opposite — a plain app with no agent and no `@theokit/*` runtime
dependencies? Scaffold with `--bare`: a Hello Theo page and the same clean
structure to grow into.

```bash
npx create-theokit my-app --bare
```

## Commands

```bash
pnpm dev          # Start dev server with HMR
pnpm build        # Build for production
pnpm start        # Run the production build
pnpm test         # Run tests (vitest)
pnpm lint         # ESLint check
pnpm typecheck    # TypeScript type check
```
